  "worked_solution": "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2xy-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{ }i+{x}^{2}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+{1}^{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\,i+j.$",
